PQC migration (whitepaper 2.2.5.C + 2.2.5.D): two flows layered on
top of the wrap envelope primitive (M4) and the sync keyring
interface (M5/M6).

- src/security/wrap-key-rotation.ts:
  - exportWrapKey / importWrapKey produce a passphrase-protected
    envelope. PBKDF2-SHA256 derives an AES-256-GCM key from the
    passphrase with 210,000 iterations (OWASP 2023 minimum). The
    envelope is base64url-encoded JSON carrying the salt, IV,
    ciphertext, authTag, keyId, iteration count, version, and
    createdAtMs; importWrapKey refuses partial output on any auth
    failure and surfaces WrapKeyBackupError for typed catches.
  - constantTimeEqual exposes Node's timingSafeEqual with a typed
    Buffer-vs-Buffer signature; mismatched lengths short-circuit
    without leaking.
  - rotateWrappingKey walks a caller-supplied list of wrapped device
    identity rows, unwraps each with the old key, and re-wraps under
    the new key. Plaintext rows are skipped and counted. The
    function is engine-agnostic (listWrapped + rewrite callbacks) so
    Doctor and tests can drive it without depending on the SQLite
    store internals.
  - WRAP_KEY_BACKUP_CONSTANTS centralises the envelope shape so CLI
    helpers (M8) and the eventual on-disk keyring metadata agree.
- src/security/wrap-key-rotation.test.ts: 20 invariants — codec
  (constantTimeEqual, constants), round-trip across multiple keys,
  weak-passphrase rejection, keyId shape validation, envelope
  tampering detection (salt/IV/authTag/version), wrong-passphrase
  AES-GCM auth failure, rotation rewrap + skip reporting, refusal
  when the old keyring is missing the seal keyId, and a 32-byte
  newKey guard before any row is touched.

20/20 green.

